<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Protest and freedom · 1938 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ey Reqîb</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ckb">ئەی ڕەقیب</bdi> / <bdi lang="ckb-Latn">Ey Řeqîb</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A poem written in 1938 by the Kurdish poet Dildar that became the anthem of the Kurdish people.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Dildar</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Kurds; the Kurdistan Republic of Mahabad; the Kurdistan Region of Iraq</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Central Kurdish (Sorani)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1938</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Dildar (1938; he died in 1948))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ey-reqib">Ey Reqîb</a>&quot; was written in 1938 in Sorani (Central Kurdish) by the Kurdish poet and political activist Dildar, while he was in jail. Its title means &quot;O Enemy!&quot;. It addresses the jail guards of the prison where Dildar was held and tortured, and beyond them the broader oppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>.</p><p>In 1946 the poem was adopted as the national anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a>, a short-lived Kurdish republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> that lasted for a year. It is known as the Kurdish national anthem and is the official anthem of the semi-autonomous Kurdistan Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. It is also used by the Kurdistan Workers' Party. Besides the official Sorani text, it is sung in other Kurdish dialects, among them Northern Kurdish (Kurmanci), Southern Kurdish and Zazaki.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>An address to an enemy: the guards who held the poet in jail and, beyond them, the oppression of the Kurdish people. The mood is defiant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Central Kurdish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Central Kurdish</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">ئەی ڕەقیب، ھەر ماوە قەومی کوردزمان،</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Ey reqîb, her mawe qewmî kurdziman,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O enemy, the Kurdish-speaking people still remains,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">نایشکێنێ دانەری تۆپی زەمان.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Nayşikênê danerî topî zeman.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the one who fires the cannon of time will not break it [?].</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">کەس نەڵێ کورد مردووە، کورد زیندووە،</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Kes nełê kurd mirdûwe, kurd zîndûwe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let no one say the Kurds are dead; the Kurds are alive,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">زیندووە، قەت نانەوێ ئاڵاکەمان.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Zîndûwe, qet nanewê ałakeman.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>alive; our flag will never be lowered.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">لاوی کورد ھەستایە سەر پێ وەک دلێر،</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Lawî kurd hestaye ser pê wek dilêr,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Kurdish youth have risen to their feet like heroes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">تا بە خوێن نەخشی بکا تاجی ژیان.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Ta be xwên nexşî bika tacî jiyan.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to paint with blood the crown of life.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">کەس نەڵێ کورد مردووە، کورد زیندووە،</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Kes nełê kurd mirdûwe, kurd zîndûwe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let no one say the Kurds are dead; the Kurds are alive,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">زیندووە، قەت نانەوێ ئاڵاکەمان.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Zîndûwe, qet nanewê ałakeman.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>alive; our flag will never be lowered.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">ئێمە ڕۆڵەی میدیا و کەیخوسرەوین،</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Ême řołey Mîdya w Keyxusrewîn,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We are the children of the Medes and of Kay Khosrow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">دینمان، ئایینمان، ھەر نیشتمان</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Dînman, ayînman, her nîştiman</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our faith, our religion is the homeland alone.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">کەس نەڵێ کورد مردووە، کورد زیندووە،</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Kes nełê kurd mirdûwe, kurd zîndûwe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let no one say the Kurds are dead; the Kurds are alive,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">زیندوە، قەت نانەوێ ئاڵاکەمان.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Zîndwe, qet nanewê ałakeman.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>alive; our flag will never be lowered.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">ئێمە ڕۆڵەی ڕەنگی سوور و شۆڕشین،</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Ême řołey řengî sûr û şořişîn,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We are the children of the red colour and of revolution,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">سەیری کە، خوێناوییە ڕابردوومان.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Seyrî ke, xwênawîye řabirdûman.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>look, how bloody our past is.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">کەس نەڵێ کورد مردووە، کورد زیندووە،</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Kes nełê kurd mirdûwe, kurd zîndûwe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let no one say the Kurds are dead; the Kurds are alive,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">زیندووە، قەت نانەوێ ئاڵاکەمان.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Zîndûwe, qet nanewê ałakeman.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>alive; our flag will never be lowered.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">لاوی کورد ھەر ‌حازر و ئامادەیە،</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Lawî kurd her hazir û amadeye,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Kurdish youth are always ready and prepared,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">گیانفیدایە، گیانفیدا، ھەر گیانفیدا.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Gyanfîdaye, gyanfîda, her gyanfîda.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>ready to give their lives, to give their lives, always to give their lives.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">کەس نەڵێ کورد مردووە، کورد زیندووە،</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Kes nełê kurd mirdûwe, kurd zîndûwe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let no one say the Kurds are dead; the Kurds are alive,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ckb">زیندووە، قەت نانەوێ ئاڵاکەمان.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ckb-Latn">Zîndûwe, qet nanewê ałakeman.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>alive; our flag will never be lowered.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Dildar (1938); the Sorani text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ey_Req%C3%AEb" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (Kurdish Latin (Hawar)).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: the Kurdish Latin (Hawar) alphabet, with ł and ř for the Sorani letters ڵ and ڕ, as Sorani writers in the Latin alphabet add them; line 3.4 keeps the text's shorter spelling زیندوە (zîndwe).</li><li>line 1.1: 'her mawe', 'still remains'; 'qewmî kurdziman', 'the people of the Kurdish tongue'.</li><li>line 1.2: 'danerî topî zeman' is read as 'the one who fires (sets) the cannon of time', i.e. the blows of time or fortune; 'daner' is literally 'the one who places or sets', and other readings ('the makers of the weapons of the age') are given.</li><li>line 1.4: 'nanewê', 'does not bow, is not lowered'.</li><li>line 2.2: 'nexşî bika', 'adorn, paint, inscribe': to inscribe the crown of life in blood.</li><li>line 3.1: Kay Khosrow (Keyxusrew), the legendary Iranian king of the Shahnameh, sometimes identified with the Median king Cyaxares.</li><li>line 5.2: 'gyanfîda', one who gives his life as a sacrifice.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
